# This repository is edited on Windows and built inside a Linux container, so
# what Git does to line endings on the way through is not cosmetic here.
#
# core.autocrlf is on for the working copy, which means Git decides per file
# whether it is text. It guesses well, but a wrong guess on a binary is silent
# and total: converting the byte 0x0A to 0x0D 0x0A inside a PNG or an ICO
# corrupts every file that happens to contain it, and the damage only appears
# after a deploy, as an icon or an image that will not render. These say
# outright which files must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
*.pptx binary
*.db  binary

# Shell scripts run inside the image. A CRLF line ending after the shebang is
# read by Linux as part of the interpreter's name, so the container dies with
# "no such file or directory" naming a binary that plainly exists. The portal
# entrypoint has already been the subject of one fix in this repository's
# history; this stops the line-ending half of that class of failure.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
